#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203852 :question/id #uuid "fd07ad0f-548a-5569-84de-d658a88c4b9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-17087" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nI. $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$\n\nII. $2z - 5 = z + 1$\n\nIII. $3z - 2 = 3z - 2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z+3 & =4z+9 \\\\ 4z+3-4z & =4z+9-4z \\\\ 3 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $z.$ Therefore, the equation $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-5 & =z+1 \\\\ 2z-5-z+5 & =z+1-z+5 \\\\ z & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2z - 5 = z + 1$ has the unique solution $z = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-2 & =3z-2 \\\\ 3z-2-3z & =3z-2-3z \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213 :topic/math-academy-id 343 :topic/title "Solving Linear Equations With Variables on Both Sides"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204600 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ccdaed-3b16-539d-8f3a-f28aba00eadc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439621} {:answer/id #uuid "51c238ba-1654-57e5-8062-656c1bcfb26e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07ccdaed-3b16-539d-8f3a-f28aba00eadc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439621} :answer-field/id #uuid "db08f348-7240-5e25-aac6-71acb1cc27dd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer